<exercisegroup>
	<title>Using the Subspace Test </title>

	<introduction><p>
		In each of the following, check each part of the <xref ref="procedure-subspaces-concepts-test" text="title" /> for subcollection <m>W</m> inside vector space <m>V</m>.
		<ul>
			<li> If you think a part of the <xref ref="procedure-subspaces-concepts-test" text="title" /> is <em>true</em>, <alert>justify it without resorting to examples</alert>. </li>
			<li> If you think a part of the <xref ref="procedure-subspaces-concepts-test" text="title" /> is <em>false</em>, <alert>provide an explicit example that demonstrates it</alert>. </li>
		</ul>
		</p></introduction>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p> <m>V=\R^2</m>, <m>W=</m>  the subcollection of vectors that are orthogonal to <m>
			   \uvec{v}=( 1,4)
			</m>.</p></statement>
		<answer><p> We can describe <m>W</m> as the subcollection of vectors <m>\uvec{u}</m> where <m>\udotprod{u}{v}=0</m>. Now we proceed with the subspace test:
<ol marker="(i)">
	<li>
		<p>
			We can demonstrate that <m>W</m> is nonempty using the zero vector <m> \zerovec=(0,0)</m>: <m>\dotprod{(0,0)}{(1,4)}=0\cdot 1+0\cdot 4=0</m>.
		</p>
	</li>
		<li>
		<p>
			If <m>\uvec{u}_1</m> and <m>\uvec{u}_2</m> are both in <m>W</m>, then <m>\dotprod{\uvec{u}_1}{\uvec{v}}=0</m> and <m>\dotprod{\uvec{u}_2}{\uvec{v}}=0</m>. We can use <xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-dot-prod-algebra-distrib-right-over-left" text="custom">distribution of the dot product over vector addition </xref> to show that <m>\uvec{u}_1+\uvec{u}_2</m> is also in <m>W</m>:
<md>
	\dotprod{(\uvec{u}_1+\uvec{u}_2)}{\uvec{v}}=\dotprod{\uvec{u}_1}{\uvec{v}}+\dotprod{\uvec{u}_2}{\uvec{v}}=0+0=0
</md>.

<m>\uvec{u}_1+\uvec{u}_2</m> is also orthogonal to <m>\uvec{v}=( 1,4)</m>, so <m>\uvec{u}_1+\uvec{u}_2</m> is also in <m>W</m>.

		</p>
	</li>
	<li>
		<p>
			Consider a vector  <m>\uvec{u}</m> in <m>W</m>, and an arbitrary scalar <m>k</m>. We can use further <xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-dot-prod-algebra-scalar-left" text="custom"> properties of the dot product </xref> to show that <m>k\uvec{u}</m> is also in <m>W</m>:
<md>
	\dotprod{(k\uvec{u})}{ \uvec{v}}=k(\udotprod{u}{v})=k(0)=0
</md>.
<m>k\uvec{u}</m> is also orthogonal to <m>\uvec{v}=( 1,4)</m>, so <m>k\uvec{u}</m> is also in <m>W</m>.

		</p>
	</li>
</ol>
All parts of the Subspace Test pass, so <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>\R^2</m>.
		
		
		
		</p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=\R^2</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of vectors <m>
			    (x,y)
			</m>
			where
			<m>
			    x^2-y^2=0
			</m>.</p></statement>
		<answer><p> 
		 We proceed with the subspace test:
<ol marker="(i)">
	<li>
		<p>
			We can demonstrate that <m>W</m> is nonempty using the zero vector <m> \zerovec=(0,0)</m>: <m>0^2-0^2=0</m>.
		</p>
	</li>
		<li>
		<p> Let's consider two arbitrary vectors 
			If <m>\uvec{u}_1=(x_1,y_1)</m> and <m>\uvec{u}_2=(x_2,y_2)</m> in <m>W</m>. Then <m>x_1^2-y_1^2=0</m> and <m>x_2^2-y_2^2=0</m>. If <m>\uvec{u}_1+\uvec{u}_2=(x_1+x_2,y_1+y_2)</m> is in <m>W</m>, then we must show that <m>(x_1+x_2)^2-(y_1+y_2)^2=0</m>. We can expand this to analyze the equation:

<md>(x_1+x_2)^2-(y_1+y_2)^2=x_1^2-y_1^2+x_2^2-y_2^2+2x_1x_2-2y_1y_2</md>. Since we're assuming <m>x_1^2-y_1^2=0</m> and <m>x_2^2-y_2^2=0</m>, this simplifies to showing that <m> 2x_1x_2-2y_1y_2=0</m>. However, MUST this hold for any choice of vectors <m>\uvec{u}_1, \uvec{u}_2</m> in <m>W</m>? </p>
 
<p> If we choose values for <m>\uvec{u}_1=(x_1,y_1)</m> and <m>\uvec{u}_2=(x_2,y_2)</m> carefully, we can see that this equation may not hold. We construct an explicit example: <m>\uvec{u}_1=(1,1)</m> and <m>\uvec{u}_2=(2,-2)</m> are both in <m>W</m>, but 
<m>\uvec{u}_1+\uvec{u}_2=(3,-1)</m> is not: <m>(3)^2-(-1)^2=8\neq 0</m>. Thus, their sum is not in the subcollection <m>W</m>, so <m>W</m> is not closed under addition and is consequently not a subspace of <m>\R^2</m>.
		</p>
	</li>
	<li>
		<p>
			Consider a vector  <m>\uvec{u}=(x,y)</m> in <m>W</m>, and an arbitrary scalar <m>k</m>. We can show that <m>k\uvec{u}=(kx,ky)</m> is also in <m>W</m>:

		<md>
		(kx)^2-(ky)^2=k^2x^2-k^2y^2=k^2(x^2-y^2)=k^2(0)=0.	
		</md>
		<m>k\uvec{u}</m> is in <m>W</m>, so <m>W</m> is closed under scalar multiplication.
		</p>
	</li>
</ol>	
		Since <m>W</m> fails closure under addition, <m>W</m> is <em>not</em> a subspace of <m>\R^2</m>.
		</p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=\R^2</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of vectors <m>
			     (x_1,x_2)
			</m>
			where
			<m>
			    x_{1}+2x_{2}\geq 0.
			</m></p></statement>
		<answer><p> We leave as an exercise for you to verify that <m>W</m> is nonempty and is closed under addition. However, <m>W</m> is <em>not</em> closed under scalar multiplication. Here is an explicit example:</p>
			<p> If we consider <m>\uvec{u}=(1,1)</m>, we can see that <m>1+2(1)=3\geq 0</m>, so <m>\uvec{u}</m> is in <m>W</m>. However, if <m>k=-2</m>, then <m>k\uvec{u}=(-2,-2)</m> is no longer in <m>W</m>: <m>-2+2(-2)=-6    \lt 0</m>. <m>W</m> is not closed under scalar multiplication, and we conclude it's not a subspace. 
		</p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

		<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=\matrixring_{2 \times 2}(\R)</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of matrices <m>A</m> where <m>A^2</m> is the zero matrix.
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p> 
		We proceed with the subspace test:
<ol marker="(i)">
	<li>
		<p>
			We can demonstrate that <m>W</m> is nonempty using the <m>2\times 2</m> zero matrix <md>\zerovec=\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}\colon \quad {\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}}^2=\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix} </md>,so <m>W</m> is nonempty.
		</p>
	</li>
		<li>
		<p> Let's consider two arbitrary vectors (that is, two <m> 2\times 2 </m> matrices).
			If <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> are in <m>W</m>, then <m>{A_1}^2=\left[\begin{smallmatrix} 0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{smallmatrix}\right]</m> and <m>{A_2}^2=\left[\begin{smallmatrix} 0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>. Must it also be true that  <md>{(A_1+A_2)}^2=\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}</md>?</p>
<p> Let's choose simple matrices for <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> and see if we can construct an example. If <m>A_1=\left[\begin{smallmatrix} 0 \amp 1\\0 \amp 0\end{smallmatrix}\right]</m> and <m>A_2=\left[\begin{smallmatrix} 0 \amp 0\\1 \amp 0\end{smallmatrix}\right]</m>, then <m>{A_1}^2</m> and <m>{A_2}^2</m> are both the zero matrix. However, <md>A_1+A_2=\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1\\1 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}</md>, and <md>{\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 1\\1 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}}^2=\begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0\\0 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}</md>, which is not the zero matrix. This shows that <m>W</m> is not closed under addition and is not a subspace of <m>V=\matrixring_{2 \times 2}(\R)</m>.

		</p>
	</li>
	<li>
		<p>
			Consider a vector  <m>A</m> in <m>W</m>, and an arbitrary scalar <m>k</m>. Now we consider <m>kA</m>:
			<md>
			{(kA)}^2=k^2A^2=k^2\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}	
			</md>.
			This means that <m>kA</m> is also in <m>W</m>, so <m>W</m> is closed under scalar multiplication. 
		</p>
	</li>
</ol>	
		Since <m>W</m> fails closure under addition, <m>W</m> is not a subspace.
			
		
		
		
		</p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

		<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=\matrixring_{2 \times 2}(\R)</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of matrices <m>A</m> where <m>\det(A)\geq 0</m>.
			</p></statement>
	
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=\matrixring_{2 \times 2}(\R)</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of matrices <m>A</m> where <m>AX=XA</m> for <m>X=\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2\\2 \amp 4\end{bmatrix}</m>.
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p> 
		 We proceed with the subspace test:
<ol marker="(i)">
	<li>
		<p>
			We can demonstrate that <m>W</m> is nonempty using the zero matrix:
			
			<md> \zerovec X=\begin{bmatrix}0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2\\2 \amp 4\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 2\\2 \amp 4\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}0 \amp 0\\0 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}=X\zerovec</md>.
		</p>
	</li>
		<li>
		<p> Let <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> be two matrices in <m>W</m>. Then it holds that <m>A_1X=XA_1</m> and <m>A_2X=XA_2</m>. We want to show that <m>A_1+A_2</m> is in <m>W</m>, in other words, <m>(A_1+A_2)X=X(A_1+A_2)</m>. We do this by using matrix algebra properties as well as the fact that <m>A_1</m> and <m>A_2</m> are both members of <m>W</m>:

		<md>(A_1+A_2)X=A_1X+A_2X=XA_1+XA_2=X(A_1+A_2)</md>	</p>
	</li>
	<li>
		<p>
		Let's show that <m>W</m> is closed under scalar multiplication. Let <m>A</m> be a matrix in <m>W</m>, and let <m>k</m> be a scalar. We know that <m>AX=XA</m> must hold, and we wish to show that <m>(kA)X=X(kA)</m> also holds. To show this, we note that because scalar multiplication can be done in any order, we have that
		<md>(kA)X=k(AX)=k(XA)=X(kA)</md>. 
		</p>
	</li>
</ol>	
	All parts of the Subspace Test pass, so <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>\matrixring_{2 \times 2}(\R)</m>.
	</p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p><m>V=\matrixring_{2 \times 2}(\R)</m>, <m>W=</m> the subcollection of upper triangular matrices.
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p> We leave this as an exercise. You will find that <m>W</m> does pass the Subspace Test, and is consequently a subspace of <m>\matrixring_{2 \times 2}(\R)</m>. </p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

		<exercise>
		<statement><p>
			<m>V=\poly_2(\R)</m>,  <m> W=</m> the subcollection of polynomials where <m>p(1)\geq 0</m> (recall: <m>p(1)</m> means to evaluate the polynomial <m>p(x)</m> at <m>x=1</m>).
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p>  Note that if <m>p(x)=ax^2+bx+c</m> is in <m>W</m>, then <m>p(1)=a(1)^2+b(1)+c=a+b+c\geq 0</m> . We can then describe <m> W</m> as the subcollection of polynomials <m>p(x)=ax^2+bx+c</m> where <m>a+b+c\geq 0</m>. We now proceed with the subspace test:
		<ol marker="(i)">
	<li>
		<p>
			We can demonstrate that <m>W</m> is nonempty using the zero polynomial <m>p(x)=0=0x^2+0x+0</m>. It holds that <m>0+0+0\geq 0</m>.
		</p>
	</li>
		<li>
		<p>
		Suppose <m>p_1</m> and <m>p_2</m> are two polynomials in this subcollection. We can write
			<md><mrow>
				p_1(x) \amp= a_1 x^2 + b_1 x + c_1, \amp
				p_2(x) \amp= a_2 x^2 + b_2 x + c_2
			</mrow></md>.

		with <m>a_1+b_1+c_1\geq 0</m> and <m>a_2+b_2+c_2\geq 0</m>. Now we consider <md>p_1(x) + p_2(x)= (a_1 + a_2) x^2 + (b_1 + b_2) x + (c_1 + c_2) </md>.
	
		We want to check that the sum of the coefficients of <m>p_1(x) + p_2(x)</m> is non-negative. We have that
<md>(a_1 + a_2)+ (b_1 + b_2) + (c_1 + c_2)=(a_1+b_1+c_1)+(a_2+b_2+c_2)\geq 0+0=0</md>.

This shows that <m>p_1(x) + p_2(x)</m> is in <m>W</m>.

		
		
			</p>
		
	</li>
	<li>
		<p>
		Now we consider a polynomial <m>p(x)</m> in <m>W</m> and a scalar <m>k</m>. If <m>p(1)\geq 0</m>, must it be true that <m>(kp)(1)=kp(1)\geq 0</m>?

		Let's construct an explicit example to demonstrate that this may not hold. If <m>p(x)=x,</m> then <m>p(1)=1\geq 0</m>. However, if we choose  <m>k=-1</m> (or any other negative scalar), then we have <m>q(x)=(-1)p(x)=-x</m>, and <m>q(1)=-1 \lt 0</m>. This means that <m>(-1)p(x)</m> is not in <m>W</m>, and <m> W</m> is not closed under scalar multiplication.

		</p>
	</li>
</ol>	
	We conclude that <m>W</m> is not a subspace,  since it is not closed under scalar multiplication. 
		
		</p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

			<exercise>
		<statement><p><em> (Calculus Required) </em></p>
			<p>
			<m>V=\poly_2(\R)</m>,  <m> W=</m> the subcollection of polynomials where <md>\int_{-1}^{1} p(x)dx=0</md>.
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p> We leave this as an exercise to show that <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>V=\poly_2(\R)</m> .</p>

			<hint><p> Use the linearity properties of integration that you learned in calculus. </p></hint>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

	<exercise>
		<statement><p><em> (Calculus Required) </em></p>
			<p>
			<m>V=\poly_2(\R)</m>,  <m> W=</m> the subcollection of polynomials where <m>p'(0)+p'(1)=0</m> (recall: <m>p'(x)=\frac{d}{dx}p(x)</m> is the derivative of <m>p(x)</m>).
			</p></statement>
		<answer><p>	 We proceed with the subspace test:
<ol marker="(i)">
	<li>
		<p>
			We can demonstrate that <m>W</m> is nonempty using the zero polynomial <m>\zerovec(x)=0</m>: 
			<md>\zerovec'(0)+\zerovec'(1)=0+0=0</md>.
		</p>
	</li>
		<li>
		<p> Let's consider two arbitrary polynomials
			If <m>p_1</m> and <m>p_2</m> in <m>W</m>. Then <m>p_1'(0)+p_1'(1)=0</m> and <m>p_2'(0)+p_2'(1)=0</m>. We can use the sum rule for derivatives to show that <m>p_1+p_2</m> is also in <m>W</m>:

<md>
	<mrow>(p_1+p_2)'(0)+(p_1+p_2)'(1) \amp= p_1'(0)+p_2'(0)+p_1'(1)+p_2'(1)</mrow>
	<mrow> \amp=(p_1'(0)+p_1'(1))+(p_2'(0)+p_2'(1)) </mrow>
	<mrow> \amp =0+0=0. </mrow>
</md>
			Since the sum <m>p_1+p_2</m> also satisfies the condition of <m>W</m>,  <m>p_1+p_2</m> is also in <m>W</m>.
		</p>
	</li>
	<li>
		<p>
			Suppose <m>p</m> is in <m>W</m>. It must be true that <m>p'(0)+p'(1)=0</m>. Then  for every scalar <m>k</m>, by the constant multiple rule of derivatives, we have
			<md>(kp)'(0)+(kp)'(1)=kp'(0)+kp'(1)=k(p'(0)+p'(1))=k(0)=0</md>

		
		Since the scalar multiple <m>kp</m> is also in <m>W</m>, <m>W</m> is closed under scalar multiplication.
		</p>
	</li>
</ol>	
All parts of the Subspace Test pass, so <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>\poly_2(\R)</m>.			
		
		
		
		</p>
		</answer>
	</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
